    "body_en": "<p>Finding dim exoplanets hidden in the glare of their stars is an ongoing challenge in astronomy. Telescopes use coronagraphs to block out starlight, but even they have their limitations. Researchers have developed a new technique with the. The institutional report frames the development in practical terms and ties it to the broader mission or observing effort.</p><p>That matters because exoplanet science has moved beyond the era of simple discovery into a period of comparative characterization. With more than five thousand confirmed planets known, the scientifically productive questions now concern atmospheric composition, internal structure, orbital history and the statistical properties of populations rather than the existence of individual worlds. A new detection or spectral measurement is most valuable when it adds a well-constrained data point to those comparative frameworks, not when it stands alone as an anecdote. Developed a new technique with the JWST that can find dim exoplanets more easily than ever. These capabilities have helped the JWST study some tough subjects hidden in the glare of stars, like debris disks, and to observe extremely luminous objects like quasars.</p><p>The JWST has fine guidance sensors and a 5-layer sunshield to raise this threshold, but the limit is still there. New research in Nature explains how astronomers developed a way to subtract more starlight from the JWST&#x27;s observations and image exoplanets that are up against the telescope&#x27;s.</p><p>Exoplanet imaging is the main method for detailed characterization of planets orbiting beyond 1 au (astronomical unit) but it requires suppressing the bright glare of the star to. “For the past 20 years, astronomers have tackled this problem by observing a different star and using its image as a reference,” said Wang said.</p><p>Their answer is a new method based on physics and modelling that also borrows ideas from machine-learning. The new system is called FLIP, (Physics-based Learning for Imaging Planets) and it overcomes the problem of switching from reference images to real observations.</p><p>The broader interest lies in making the target less anecdotal and more comparable with the rest of the known planetary population. Population-level questions, such as the frequency of atmospheres around small rocky planets or the prevalence of water-rich worlds in the habitable zone, require well-characterized individual data points before statistical patterns become meaningful. Each new planet with a measured radius, mass and, ideally, atmospheric constraint is a brick in that larger structure, and the accumulation of bricks eventually allows theorists to test formation models against real distributions rather than projections.</p><p>In essence, the researchers created what they&#x27;re calling a &quot;digital twin&quot; of the JWST with FLIP. They tested it on 51 Eridani b, an exoplanet found with ground-based telescopes.</p><p>Because the account originates with Universe Today, it functions best as a primary institutional report that is close to the data and operations, not as independent scientific validation. Institutional communications are produced by organizations with legitimate interests in presenting their work in a favorable light, which does not make them unreliable but does make them partial. Details that complicate the narrative, including instrument limitations, unexpected failures and results below projections, tend to be minimized relative to progress messages. Technical documentation and peer-reviewed publications, where they exist, provide the complementary layer that institutional releases cannot substitute.</p><p>The next step is to improve independent constraints on the mass, radius, atmospheric composition and orbital dynamics of the target. Transmission spectroscopy with JWST, radial velocity campaigns with high-resolution ground-based spectrographs and phase-curve measurements from space photometry represent the observational toolkit that can move characterization from plausible to robust. That convergence of techniques is the standard the community now expects before a planetary atmosphere result is treated as confirmed.</p><p class=\"art-source\"><a href=\"https://www.universetoday.com/articles/exoplanets-cant-hide-from-the-jwsts-digital-twin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Source</a></p>",
